      "card_ids": ["C21"],
      "claim": "Para-aminobenzoic acid, a close structural analog of the p-aminophenylsulfonamide core, has an oral bioavailability of 90.1 ± 8.7% in humans, supporting high absorption for the query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C18"],
      "claim": "Para-aminosalicylic acid, another p-aminobenzoic acid-like small molecule, shows 50–60% oral bioavailability in humans, supporting a high F for the query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C06"],
      "claim": "Tolfenamic acid, a small oral aromatic drug with an aniline/carboxylate motif, has absolute oral bioavailability of 63.2% in healthy volunteers, supporting high F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C16"],
      "claim": "Amrinone, a small heteroaromatic drug, has oral bioavailability of 90–95% in healthy subjects, supporting high F for a small polar aromatic molecule.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C23"],
      "claim": "Acalabrutinib, a small oral drug with absolute bioavailability of 25%, demonstrates that even lower-end small-molecule F values remain above the 20% threshold, supporting a high prediction.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "All retrieved human oral bioavailability data for close structural and physicochemical analogues are above the 20% threshold, with most values substantially higher (38–95%). The query is a small, well-absorbed sulfonamide-like molecule, and no affirmative evidence supports low bioavailability.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for the exact query molecule was retrieved.",
    "Some analogues have F values near the threshold (23–38%), so the exact margin above 20% for the query is not directly quantified."
  ],
